 ### Monorepo / Multi-Composer-Root Support
 
-When the workspace root has no `composer.json` but contains subdirectories that are independent Composer projects, PHPantom discovers and processes each subproject automatically. This is a best-effort mitigation for workspaces where the editor opens a monorepo root as a single folder. The monorepo path only activates when there is no root `composer.json`.
-
-**Discovery.** `discover_subproject_roots` in `composer.rs` walks the workspace using the `ignore` crate (`WalkBuilder`), respecting `.gitignore` at every level. Any directory containing a `composer.json` is recorded as a subproject root. Nested subprojects (a `composer.json` inside an already-accepted root) are filtered out. Each discovered `composer.json` is read to extract the `config.vendor-dir` setting.
-
-**Per-subproject processing.** For each discovered `(subproject_root, vendor_dir)`, the same Composer pipeline runs: PSR-4 mappings (with base paths resolved to absolute paths so `resolve_class_path` works regardless of workspace root), classmap parsing, vendor package scanning from `installed.json`, and autoload file indexing. Results are merged into the shared backend state with first-subproject-wins semantics for duplicate FQNs.
-
-**Loose file discovery.** After all subprojects are processed, the gitignore-aware full-scan walker (`scan_workspace_fallback_full`) runs on the workspace root with a skip set containing all subproject root paths. This picks up PHP files outside any subproject tree (e.g. top-level scripts, shared utilities) without double-scanning subproject content.
-
-**Vendor tracking.** Each subproject's vendor directory is registered in both `vendor_uri_prefixes` (for URI-level vendor detection in diagnostics, find references, and rename) and `vendor_dir_paths` (for filesystem-level skip logic in go-to-implementation and workspace indexing).
-
-**Trade-offs.** Conflicting class versions across subprojects result in first-wins resolution. A single PHP version is used for the entire workspace. Per-subproject `.phpantom.toml` is not supported. Multi-root LSP workspaces (separate `workspaceFolders`) are a separate feature.
+The LSP transport uses `ProjectRouter` (`src/project_router.rs`) to select an independent `Backend` for each Composer project. Workspace folders without a root `composer.json` also use `discover_subproject_roots`; discovery respects ignore rules and does not recursively accept Composer projects inside an already accepted project. A containing workspace context excludes its child project roots and serves loose files. This discovery policy does not imply support for every nested Composer layout. The older merged discovery helpers remain available to direct `Backend` callers; the isolation boundary described here is the routed LSP service.
+
+**Project-local semantics.** Each context is freshly constructed, with its own symbol indexes, imports, parsed declarations, reference indexes, derived caches, PHP target and configuration. Worker/request clones share only their own context's state. Existing class/function/type resolvers run unchanged within that context; there is no mutable process-wide project switch. A dependency shared by two apps is resolved through each app's graph, rather than through another app's FQN winner. Watch events are filtered by consumers; Composer topology and workspace-folder changes retire and rebuild contexts.
+
+**Shared source, independent facts.** `SourceAuthority` (`src/source_authority.rs`) groups physical sources by canonical symlink identity, while preserving each client's URI, actual text and version. Opening a passive alias does not replace an active source; the latest accepted edit selects the preferred alias. Class, function and constant candidate publication follows that physical-source owner, including removal of symbols. Different physical files retain deterministic duplicate precedence within one project. Dependency reads follow the preferred overlay. Direct requests against a different alias require both open client snapshots to have identical text; otherwise they return content-modified instead of interpreting one buffer's coordinates against another's bytes. Hard links are not unified.
+
+**Source lifecycle.** The router serializes accepted source events against request admission and records each open URI's consumers and physical identity. A retargeted symlink retires old-only consumers without rereading its new target into them; new consumers receive the full current document and real version before later incremental edits. Old and new physical groups reselect their declarations and derived indexes. Closed alias observations are revalidated on filesystem updates. Watcher changes expand across observed physical aliases; open client snapshots remain authoritative until accepted edits or final close. Public LSP tests cover watched changes, deletion and closed-alias retargeting.
+
+**Deferred and navigation identity.** Completion, action, lens and hierarchy payloads retain an opaque context token around their original data. Resolution validates that context and refuses retired tokens. Builtin locations include `project_context` in their virtual URI so opening a declaration retains the selected target's stub corpus. Navigation records the consuming context for shared dependency documents; later navigation intent supersedes older replies. Workspace-wide listings aggregate contexts rather than merging their semantic maps. Routes, views and active-target queries accept a document URI; an omitted URI requires an unambiguous context.
+
+**Diagnostic identity.** `ProjectScope` limits delivery to the selected owner and excludes child roots; physical authority additionally suppresses passive aliases. Semantic computation and source revision fences remain context-local. `TransportClock` shares only the client-opening ledger and monotonic publication sequence across children and rebuilds. Actual client opens advance the ledger; child replay seeds that same opening without inventing a client reopen. Passive clears carry the actual version, opening, completeness and sequence envelope expected by the host. Pull result IDs are context-qualified. Retired contexts stop diagnostic delivery, their workers are signalled, and watcher registrations and progress tokens are context-namespaced.
+
+Discovery of independent projects nested inside an accepted Composer root remains a separate [backlog item](todo/indexing.md#x19-discover-independent-nested-composer-roots-inside-composer-workspaces). Physical identity follows canonical paths and symlinks; distinct hard-link paths are not unified.
 
